igate while eating normally and curious about freedom from negotiation rather than bracing for a stricter control system.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Assigned transition verdict:** The chapter successfully moves the reader from expecting discipline, deprivation, and permanent management to accepting a low-risk investigation conducted without immediate restriction. Recognition is specific and nonjudgmental, the managed-truce comparison makes the alternative experiential rather than merely behavioral, and the promise is kept conditional enough that later benefit-demolition work remains available. By the end, the reader understands that the proposed destination is not increasingly perfect control but no longer wanting or planning BAD SUGAR as a special reward—“without deprivation, without battle, and without a lifetime of rules.” The full instruction arrives after that distinction has been established, so continuing to eat normally feels integral to the method rather than like a postponement.\n\n**Largest momentum loss:** NONE"},"sequence_number":499}

